  , Tuple "elevator" "{\"glassbox\":2,\"id\":\"elevator\",\"title\":\"Elevator\",\"notes\":[\"Written to try to BREAK the format, not to flatter it. The looper and the\",\"car radio were both authored by whoever designed the format, so of course\",\"they fitted. An elevator is the first machine that pushes back.\",\"\",\"The thing it pushes on is floors. A twelve-storey lift plainly has more to\",\"it than a three-storey one, but 'which floor am I on' is NOT a mode: the\",\"lift behaves identically on floor 2 and floor 7, and making each floor a\",\"state would give twelve copies of one machine and an unreadable drawing.\",\"\",\"So the floor number lives in the HOST, and reaches the machine as facts \\u2014\",\"is anything requested above me, below me, here. That is how real lift\",\"controllers are written, and the format's own sorting test agrees: change\",\"the current floor and the diagram does not move, so it is not part of the\",\"machine. Change 'doors need holding' and it does.\",\"\",\"The cost is that a designer has to know to do this, and nothing in the\",\"format tells them. That is a finding, not a defect.\",\"\",\"It pushed back a second time over commands, and taught the same lesson in\",\"a different key. The obvious move is 'leaving doors-open closes the\",\"doors' \\u2014 and it is wrong, because doors-open is adjacent to doors-held,\",\"which wants them left open. Doors are closed on ENTRY to each of the\",\"states that require them shut instead. The general rule the corpus keeps\",\"arriving at: entry commands say what must now be true and compose safely;\",\"exit commands are only safe for something no neighbouring state can want,\",\"which here is exactly one thing \\u2014 the alarm.\"],\"initial\":\"idle\",\"clock\":\"seconds\",\"states\":[{\"id\":\"idle\",\"label\":\"Idle\",\"entry\":[\"close-doors\",\"hold-brake\"]},{\"id\":\"up\",\"label\":\"Going up\",\"entry\":[\"close-doors\",\"drive-up\"]},{\"id\":\"down\",\"label\":\"Going down\",\"en